    <statement><p>Using the graph of the rational function <m>y = f(x)</m> given in the figure below, evaluate the limits.  If you need to enter <m>\infty</m> or <m>- \infty</m>, type INFINITY or -INFINITY.</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__LoyolaChicago__Precalc__Chap9Sec5__Q15-image-1.png" width="50%"/>
<p>(a) <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>(b) <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to - \infty} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>(c) <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1^+} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p>(d) <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1^-} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>5</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>5</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\infty </m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\infty </m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><alert>  SOLUTION </alert>



There is a horizontal asymptote at <m>y = 5</m>, so
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} f(x) = 5</m> and <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to - \infty} f(x) = 5</m>.



There is a vertical asymptote at <m>x = 1</m> such that
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1^+} f(x) = \infty</m> and <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1^-} f(x) = \infty</m>.



</solution>
